{"name":"Charred Hickory","hex":"#5b4e4a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"charred","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of char"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["burnt, carbonized"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/charred"},{"term":"hickory","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various deciduous hardwood trees of the genera Carya and Annamocarya, one species of which, Carya illinoinensis, is the source of pecan nuts.","The wood of these trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hickory"}],"entries":[{"title":"Charring","blocks":[{"text":"Charring is a chemical process of incomplete combustion of certain solids when subjected to high heat. Heat distillation removes water vapour and volatile organic compounds (syngas) from the matrix. The residual black carbon material is char, as distinguished from the lighter colored ash. By the action of heat, charring removes hydrogen and oxygen from the solid, so that the remaining char is composed primarily of carbon. Most solid organic compounds, like wood or biological tissue, and thermoset polymers exhibit charring behaviour. In non-scientific terms, charring means partially burning so as to blacken the surface."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charring","wikidata":"Q5086659"},{"title":"Hickory","blocks":[{"text":"Hickory is a common name for trees composing the genus Carya, which includes 19 species accepted by Plants of the World Online."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Carya_Morton_29-U-10.jpg/330px-Carya_Morton_29-U-10.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hickory","wikidata":"Q142788"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Adam_ne_yap%C4%B1yor%28k%C3%B6m%C3%BCr_yap%C4%B1yor%29_-_panoramio.jpg/500px-Adam_ne_yap%C4%B1yor%28k%C3%B6m%C3%BCr_yap%C4%B1yor%29_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Adam_ne_yap%C4%B1yor%28k%C3%B6m%C3%BCr_yap%C4%B1yor%29_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Adam_ne_yap%C4%B1yor%28k%C3%B6m%C3%BCr_yap%C4%B1yor%29_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Adam_ne_yap%C4%B1yor%28k%C3%B6m%C3%BCr_yap%C4%B1yor%29_-_panoramio.jpg/500px-Adam_ne_yap%C4%B1yor%28k%C3%B6m%C3%BCr_yap%C4%B1yor%29_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A charcoal burner tending his charcoal clamp, in which he is producing charcoal. The process takes days, and the clamp has to be monitored to stop the fire from breaking out (technically, the oxygen from breaking in).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdam_ne_yap%C4%B1yor(k%C3%B6m%C3%BCr_yap%C4%B1yor)_-_panoramio.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Art_House_Project03s3872.jpg/500px-Art_House_Project03s3872.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Art_House_Project03s3872.jpg/1280px-Art_House_Project03s3872.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Art_House_Project03s3872.jpg/500px-Art_House_Project03s3872.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Traditional houses in Naoshima, Kagawa clad with yakisugi panels","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArt_House_Project03s3872.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Charred_Sweet_Potato.jpg/500px-Charred_Sweet_Potato.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c